From 70ddb2a39380605c8b0d928f15736489a14d86c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Riccardo Manfrin <3090891+riccardomanfrin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 10:54:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 02/15] [client] Bound the Signal stream registration wait and
 track its state based on stream health (#8175)

* [client] Move the Signal registration wait into its own helper

Pure move, no behavior change. The wait for the registration header and
the check on its value leave connect() for awaitRegistration(), so the
next commit can bound that wait in one place.

* [client] Split the Signal test server helper from its listener setup

Pure move, no behavior change. serveTestSignalExchange serves any
SignalExchangeServer on a local listener; startTestSignalServer keeps
serving the real server through it. Lets a test wrap the real server.

* [client] Bound the wait for the Signal registration header

After an outage the Signal client could wait forever for the registration
header of a new stream. Keepalive cannot end that wait when whatever
answers the transport (a proxy in front of Signal, or Signal itself)
accepts the stream and never answers it, and the receive watchdog only
starts once registration succeeds. Signal then stayed down until the
daemon was restarted, while Management and Relay recovered.

Cancel the stream when the header does not arrive within 10 seconds, so
connect() fails and the existing retry loop opens a new stream. A timer
is used instead of a context deadline because the stream context must
outlive registration.

* [client] Report Signal unhealthy while no stream is registered

IsHealthy judged Signal by the gRPC connection state alone and marked it
connected whenever the connection was Ready or Idle. With the connection
up but no stream registered, the client cannot receive offers or answers,
yet netbird status showed Signal: Connected, hiding a stuck registration
for as long as it lasted.

Require a registered stream before reporting healthy. When there is none,
return false without touching the recorder: the disconnect that dropped
the stream already reported its reason.

* [client] Name the server that reconnected in the SR watcher log

Signal and Relay shared one reconnect callback, which logged "reconnected
to Signal or Relay server" for either. A Relay reconnect while the Signal
stream was down read as Signal having recovered, which misled diagnosis
of a stuck Signal registration.

Register one callback per server so the log names the one that actually
reconnected. Which listeners get notified, and when, is unchanged.

* [client] Trim comments in the Signal registration and health code

Comment-only change, no behavior change. Drop comments that restate the
code and keep only the reason a timer is used instead of a context
deadline.

* [client] Check Signal stream registration before the connection state

IsHealthy returned true for a Connecting or Shutdown channel before the
registration check ran, so a client with no registered stream could
still be reported healthy while the channel was reconnecting. Check the
stream first; the connection-state cases now only apply to a client
that has a registered stream.

* [client] Log a plain connect in the SR watcher

The Signal and Relay callbacks also fire on the first connection, not
only after a disconnect, so "reconnected" was wrong at startup. Log
"connected to <server> server" for both cases.

* [client] Say what the SR watcher does when it logs

The line was read as a per-server connect log, which Signal and Relay
already emit themselves. What it marks is the peer guards being told to
reset their reconnection backoff, which happens only once both are
ready. Log that, naming the server whose event triggered it.
