From 16b04510de7c36086788d0356b4885f59dd8d32c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: riccardom <riccardomanfrin@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:41:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/15] [client] Hold a never-direct HA routing peer before a
 direct peer takes over

The peers of an HA group come up over relay first and turn direct one after
the other, as when lazy connections wake the group or the client reconnects.
A routing peer that was relayed when it carried the route gave way at once
to the first equivalent peer that turned direct, so in a group of N peers
the route moved right after the session started in roughly (N-1)/N of the
cases, resetting every masqueraded flow, typically an SSH session opened on
the wake-up traffic.

Such a peer is now kept for the relayedSwitch delay, counted from the first
evaluation that finds a direct contender. If it turns direct within the
delay the route stays; otherwise it moves when the delay runs out, so the
route does not stay on a relay for longer than the delay. A peer that
dropped from direct keeps counting from the drop.
